VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: DEI-040
Reason for Visit: Acute presentation of a severe panic attack, uncontrollable bruxism resulting in a fractured tooth, and profound sleep deprivation.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient and spouse
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage noted sudden onset of severe facial pain, intractable hyperventilation, and a 72-hour period of complete insomnia driven by political news consumption. Immediate out-of-hours face-to-face assessment was required to manage the acute autonomic crisis, assess the dental injury, and break the severe insomnia cycle prior to routine GP availability.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: I can't breathe, my jaw just cracked from clenching so hard, and I haven't slept in three days because the world is literally ending.
History of Present Illness: The patient describes a chronic history of intrusive legislative rumination regarding current election cycles. Over the past 72 hours, an escalation in socio-political news triggered extreme doom-scrolling insomnia. The patient experienced a visceral, news-triggered panic response tonight characterized by severe hyperventilation and uncontrollable somatic tension (bruxism), resulting in the fracture of a molar and acute facial pain. The patient reports strained relationships with colleagues holding opposing views, feelings of profound helplessness regarding systemic justice, a diminished capacity to experience joy, avoidance of public spaces, and sudden bursts of tearfulness linked directly to media consumption.
Functional Status: Acutely functionally impaired due to severe sleep deprivation, acute facial pain, and overwhelming panic.
Social/Safety Concerns: High risk of severe physiological exhaustion and dental injury due to completely unmanaged anxiety and chronic doom-scrolling.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 150/95 | HR: 115 | Temp: 36.8 | RR: 24 | SpO2: 98% | Wt: 75 Kg
General: Patient is highly agitated, pacing, holding their jaw, and breathing rapidly. The spouse is attempting to turn off various news alerts.
Physical Exam: Cardiovascular: Tachycardia, regular rhythm. Respiratory: Tachypneic, clear lung fields. Musculoskeletal/Dental: Severe, bilateral spasm of the masseter and temporalis muscles. Intraoral exam reveals a visible, non-displaced fracture of the lower right second molar, sensitive to air/touch. No active bleeding or airway compromise. Neurological: Cranial nerves intact. Alert and oriented, but exhibits pressured speech and severe hyperarousal. Legislative rumination dominates the exam; the patient repeatedly attempts to show the clinician news articles on their phone to justify their panic.
Environmental Observations: Two televisions and a tablet are all actively displaying different 24-hour news channels; the environment is highly chaotic.
Medication Reconciliation: No regular prescription medications. Taking paracetamol with minimal relief for jaw pain.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Socio-Political Climate Anxiety - Adjustment Disorder (with Anxiety) - V62.89 - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Acute Coronary Syndrome - Ruled out/unlikely due to the localized jaw pain originating strictly from the fractured tooth and masseter spasm, normal oxygen saturation, and absence of central ischemic chest pressure.

Trigeminal Neuralgia - Ruled out/unlikely as the facial pain is a constant, severe ache with localized dental sensitivity related to acute trauma (bruxism), rather than spontaneous, electric-shock type pain.

Primary Manic Episode - Ruled out/unlikely as the insomnia and rapid speech are explicitly driven by intense, reality-based fear and media consumption (doom-scrolling), lacking grandiosity or euphoria.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Moderate

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presented with an acute autonomic panic response (HR 115, BP 150/95), severe somatic muscle tension resulting in acute dental trauma, and a dangerous 72-hour period of complete insomnia driven by socio-political anxiety. Immediate out-of-hours face-to-face assessment was critical to physically examine the airway and dental injury, definitively exclude radiating cardiac pain mimicking jaw pain, and provide urgent pharmacological intervention to break the acute panic and insomnia cycles. Delaying care until routine hours would have perpetuated severe pain and psychological crisis, firmly justifying urgent medical intervention.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Administered oral diazepam 5mg to rapidly reduce the sympathetic overdrive, relax the severe masseter spasm, and initiate sleep. Prescribed oral ibuprofen 400mg for acute dental inflammation. Instructed the spouse to unplug all televisions. Advised the usual GP to arrange psychometric validation using tailored eco-anxiety or socio-political stress scales to formally document the functional impairment.
Positive Behavioral Change: To permanently break the doom-scrolling insomnia, implement a "physical device quarantine": hand all smartphones and tablets to the spouse at 7:00 PM every evening to be locked in another room; physically removing access to the triggering stimuli is required when self-control is exhausted. To manage the acute legislative rumination during the day, utilize a "structured worry period": allocate exactly 15 minutes at noon to read the news, setting a loud, un-ignorable alarm to end the session; restricting the consumption to a specific time prevents the anxiety from bleeding into the entire day.
Home-Based Services (Optional): Referral to an emergency dentist for the fractured molar and a psychologist specializing in systemic anxiety.
DME/Safety (Optional): Dental review for a fitted night guard.
Follow-up: Urgent dental review within 24 hours. Routine GP review within 72 hours to assess blood pressure and sleep quality.

